{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Make for yourself a saraf.</b> The word <b>for yourself</b> — our Sages of blessed memory said (Avodah Zarah 44a), and this is their language: ״Behold, it says 'for yourself' — from your own, and a person cannot prohibit a thing that is not his,״ end quote. Meaning, since Moshe made it, it is not prohibited when future generations worship it. And we learn this matter from what we see after the truth [i.e., from the outcome]; but it is far-fetched to say that HaShem told him to make it from his own specifically so that it would not become prohibited through worship by future generations, and one needs to know why HaShem so commanded. Further, there is reason to note why HaShem said to him 'a saraf,' while in the narrative Moshe calls it 'a serpent.' Further, one needs to know the reason for this matter — that HaShem commanded to make a serpent and to gaze upon it: what does gazing at a serpent accomplish to heal the bite? And our Sages of blessed memory said (Rosh HaShanah 29a): ״All the while that Israel gazed upward,״ etc., end quote. And if the intent in this was to gaze upward alone, it would have been more fitting to gaze at the heavens, and then the intent would be more evident to the one doing it than through gazing at a serpent.\"" "<b>And it appears</b> according to what I have explained, that HaShem punished them with the seraph serpents corresponding to two offenses they committed: that they spoke against Elohim and against Moshe. Therefore the answer came from HaShem and He said to him <b>Make for yourself a saraf</b> — here He hinted that he should make the serpent in an arrangement such that the two particulars of the sin would be hinted at, namely that they spoke against HaShem and spoke against Moshe. Corresponding to what they spoke against Moshe He said <b>Make for yourself</b> — meaning, corresponding to the matter that touches you. And corresponding to the particular of the sin that they spoke against HaShem He said <b>a saraf</b> — meaning, in the likeness of a saraf. For there was a need in the matter that it contain a recognition of two things, which are the evil speech they spoke against Elohim and also what they spoke against Moshe, for a reason that I shall explain.\"" "And Moshe devised to make a recognition for the species of the sin they committed, which is the aspect of the serpent, through making it from copper (נחושת), for without this there is no recognition of both particulars of the sin in the likeness of the saraf. And this is what is said <b>and Moshe made a serpent (נחש)</b> — meaning, this particular of serpent, which is a recognition of the particular of the sin they committed, he made as a saraf from copper (נחושת) and not from something else. It is found that the model of the serpent corresponds to the recognition of the saraf, for it resembles it in form, and the material from which it was made, which is the copper (נחושת), corresponds to the recognition of the particular of the serpent (נחש) from which it is. \\\"" "<b>And the reason</b> for the entire matter appears upon raising yet another question: why did the prayer of Moshe not avail to remove the seraph serpents so that they would not bite, necessitating a remedy for them? Rather, this surely declares that the Master of Justice examined them and found that they had not done repentance sufficient for them. And although they said ״We have sinned, for we have spoken against HaShem and against you,״ the main thing is lacking, which is the abandonment [of the sin], and we do not find that they said ״We shall not return again to this evil thing״; and for this the affliction was not removed. Therefore the Creator devised wisely and took counsel to remedy them in this manner: for whoever mends himself properly — behold, he is saved and no serpent will bite him; and whoever does not mend himself as required — a serpent will bite him, to stir him to repentance. And He commanded him: to look upon a serpent, etc. — and through this he will mend himself and repent, and it will heal him.\"" "<b>And when you gaze</b> upon the serpent — it alludes to seven things:\"" "<b>First</b>, the answer along this line: Behold, what causes the serpent to bite? It is the sin of human beings, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 10:11): ״אִם יִשֹּׁךְ הַנָּחָשׁ בְּלֹא לָחַשׁ״ — and when there is no incantation, the serpent becomes inanimate, like the serpent that they would look upon when bitten by a serpent. And this is the implication of their gazing at the inanimate serpent — to say: ״אֶל זֶה״ — to this I look, to do as its deed, to be still from speaking further; and through this they accomplish abandoning the sin." "<b>Second</b>, to recognize their sin, along the lines of what is said in the Zohar (Tikkunei Zohar 13) concerning that serpent of cunning in the tower — which is Samael, likened to the serpent that first seduced man. And this is the significance of placing the serpent in an elevated place: that they should recognize that they were the cause, in that they spoke lashon hara to bring the serpent into an elevated place. Also, that they performed the deed of the serpent — which is lashon hara — in the supernal place, in that they spoke against Elohim. There is also the implication that they spoke against Moshe when they saw a certain material from which the serpent was made." "<b>Third</b>, to impress upon them how precious supernal things are compared to earthly things, for the serpent below kills, while the supernal serpent gives life. And through this they would feel the evil of what they spoke against the manna, which came from heaven — which is the celestial bread — and their choosing the bread that the earth brings forth, concerning whom it is said (Isaiah 5:20): ״הוֹי אוֹמְרִים עַל הַטּוֹב רַע וְעַל הָרַע טוֹב״." "<b>Fourth</b>, to awaken them to repentance for their complaint as to why HaShem brought them by way of the wilderness where there is no provision, and did not bring them by way of a place where barley and bread are normally found — for HaShem desired that their eyes be turned toward Elohim who dwells in heaven, for their sustenance and for all their needs, with no reliance other than Him. For this very reason He did not remove the serpents but left them to bite, and they would be healed when they looked upward to their Father in heaven. And this is the statement of our Sages, of blessed memory (Rosh HaShanah 29a) — except that they did not spell out the implication in the matter, which is a response to the words of lashon hara they spoke about why He brought them into the wilderness: that HaShem desired this so that their hearts would be, without interruption, constantly drawn near to HaShem — like a son who awaits his father's table, evening and morning and at every time and season. And had He brought them by way of settled land, they would not have had this constant attachment, since their provision would be prepared before them. And how greatly this matter causes the heart to be distanced from recognizing the Creator's providence and the fear of His exaltedness." "<b>Fifth</b>, to respond to what was included in their complaint when they asked why He brought them through the wilderness — for their intention was that one cannot rely on a miracle, because a miracle does not endure. Go and learn from what the deniers of HaShem's wonders say: they seek false wisdoms to deny the miracle and to render the matter through nature, so that that event would be limited to that time, and every change in nature is not permanent. And it is possible that the root of this answer was already in the hearts of those misguided speakers; therefore HaShem commanded to perform a fixed miracle, as our Sages of blessed memory said (Bamidbar Rabbah 19:23), that Moshe threw the serpent skyward and it stood in the air and there established its place without support. And every person who was bitten would look upon it and recognize the falsity of the errant notion he had previously held, and believe that HaShem who brought them to the wilderness would perform miracles continuously — to the degree that miracles would function as ordinary nature, and the wilderness would be like a settled land for every need." "<b>(v)</b> To show them the power of repentance, in the manner that they of blessed memory said (Yoma 86b) that for penitents transgresses become merits — for it is not mere forgiveness of the sin, but on the contrary it stands for him as a merit. And this is what He indicated through looking at the serpent of copper — to say that the serpent bit, and it itself, when it is in the aspect of silence (meaning that it speak no more, even though it had already spoken, for every species of serpent speaks evil speech) — nonetheless, it itself gives life and is transformed from evil to good:" "<b>(vi)</b> For HaShem desires that these children recognize El in a particular way: that each one of the bitten look upward and perceive his own miracle, and thereby they will justify the particular providence." "<b>And it shall be, every one who is bitten.</b> It says <b>והיה</b> — an expression of joy over the biting, on account of the benefits drawn from looking at the serpent of copper in the seven matters by which the beneficiary of the miracle will recognize, as we have written. And it says <b>כל הנשוך</b> — meaning, even if he had already been bitten before the making of the serpent of copper. And its saying <b>וחי</b> with the added ו״ו is also to allude to the vitality of the soul as well: when he perceives in the miracle and in the particulars alluded to, he justifies himself and lives — vitality of the body and vitality of the soul."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:12:24.718827352Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}